SQL transport: more than one road, and the entity that decides who you are

TWO FIXES, BOTH ABOUT READING THE RIGHT BOOK RELIABLY.

1 · THE RELAY WAS A SINGLE POINT OF FAILURE WEARING THE COSTUME OF A FIX.
runReadOnlyQuery was a hard either/or — relayEnabled() and every live read in
the estate went down one process on one box, with no fallback. When salesmaster
restarts, and it does, every live screen in six systems goes to its unreachable
state at once and it reads as Micromart's server being down when it is not.

SERVICESUITE_RELAY_URL is now an ordered LIST. A single URL behaves exactly as
before, so nothing breaks by upgrading. Put a relay on Micromart's own SQL host
— always on, because it is the thing everyone is querying — list it first, and a
salesmaster restart costs one failed connect on the next request.

The rules that make failover safe rather than merely present:
  · A SQL error is an ANSWER. "Invalid column name" reached SQL Server and came
    back; asking a second relay gives the identical error and doubles the load.
    Only transport failures move roads.
  · WRITES NEVER MOVE. A proc that timed out may still have run — the row was
    written and the response was lost — so the retry posts a second loan against
    a real customer. exec and proc are pinned to one road and say so in the
    error when they fail.
  · Sticky. Once a road answers it is preferred, or every request pays the dead
    primary's connect timeout and one broken box becomes latency everywhere.

SERVICESUITE_SQL_DIRECT places direct TDS in that order: off (default, and
correct on Vercel where the CGNAT address has no route and connectionTimeout is
20 seconds), first (a tailnet runtime), or last.

scripts/verify-relay-failover.mts proves it against real sockets — a relay that
is not listening, one returning 503, one returning a SQL error — including that
the spare relay never sees a write. `npm run test:relay`. Eleven checks.

2 · THE ENTITY WAS RESOLVED FROM THE WRONG PLACE.
resolveOrg read `row.serviceSuiteEntityId ?? registryDefault`, so the database
column always won. prisma/seed.ts writes 3002 for micromart; connections.ts
records that a live read found Micro Eazy active under 3005 and absent from
3002, and that THE TWO BOOKS HOLD DIFFERENT PEOPLE ON THE SAME PHONE NUMBERS.

So a deployment on 3002 does not fail. It succeeds, against strangers — finds a
borrower, shows a balance, posts a loan, for somebody who is not the person
holding the phone. connections.ts already called the env var a per-deployment
override; it simply was not one, because that line outranked it. Now an
explicitly set SERVICESUITE_ENTITYID_<ORG> wins, then the column, then the
registry default. getEntityIdOverride distinguishes "a human set this" from "the
code's default", which getEntityId could not.

